WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.436 --> 00:00:11.556
So I'm going to talk to you
about carbon removals,

00:00:11.556 --> 00:00:13.556
and I’m going to start with this.

00:00:14.796 --> 00:00:17.156
This is a woolly pig.

00:00:17.396 --> 00:00:18.396
(Laughter)

00:00:18.516 --> 00:00:20.676
She's gorgeous, obviously,

00:00:20.676 --> 00:00:24.636
and she’s also one part
in a whole new set of approaches

00:00:24.636 --> 00:00:26.196
for dealing with climate change.

00:00:26.356 --> 00:00:29.636
They are all called carbon removals,

00:00:29.636 --> 00:00:32.956
and they all involve
taking CO2 out of the sky

00:00:32.956 --> 00:00:34.756
and storing it somewhere safe.

00:00:35.396 --> 00:00:37.636
I’ll come back to the woolly pig later,

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:41.236
but first, why do we need carbon removals?

00:00:42.276 --> 00:00:44.036
Because I know what you’re thinking:

00:00:44.036 --> 00:00:47.436
isn’t it easier to stop
putting the carbon in the atmosphere

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:48.436
in the first place?

00:00:48.556 --> 00:00:50.996
And the answer is yes, of course it is.

00:00:51.276 --> 00:00:54.036
We have to do everything we can
as fast as we can

00:00:54.036 --> 00:00:56.516
to stop carbon getting into the sky

00:00:56.516 --> 00:00:59.236
and stop climate change getting worse.

00:00:59.996 --> 00:01:03.476
That should not be a controversial
point of view at this meeting.

00:01:05.556 --> 00:01:08.116
But the problem is ...

00:01:08.116 --> 00:01:09.476
we’ve left it too late.

00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:14.556
We can't now do it fast enough.

00:01:15.756 --> 00:01:18.836
I've been working on climate change
for more than 20 years,

00:01:18.836 --> 00:01:21.316
and I’ve talked about it
in boardrooms and classrooms

00:01:21.316 --> 00:01:22.996
and everywhere in between,

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:25.996
and I was shocked when I found this out,

00:01:25.996 --> 00:01:28.756
but the science is utterly ...

00:01:28.756 --> 00:01:29.756
utterly clear.

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:34.236
If we’re going to have a fighting chance
of staying below 1.5 degrees,

00:01:34.236 --> 00:01:36.396
that “safe limit,”

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:39.516
we have to have carbon removals,

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:41.676
and it looks like
we’re going to need a lot.

00:01:42.996 --> 00:01:45.556
Scenarios from the Energy
Transitions Commission,

00:01:45.556 --> 00:01:47.636
from the International Energy Agency,

00:01:47.636 --> 00:01:50.036
from the Intergovernmental Panel
on Climate Change,

00:01:50.036 --> 00:01:55.276
have all shown that we’re going to need
billions of tonnes of carbon removals

00:01:55.276 --> 00:01:59.116
between now and 2050

00:01:59.116 --> 00:02:01.836
to give us a chance
of staying below 1.5 degrees.

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:06.036
We’re going to need
both reductions and removals.

00:02:07.756 --> 00:02:11.316
But there's another more heartening reason
why we need carbon removals.

00:02:11.956 --> 00:02:13.356
They also give us a chance --

00:02:13.356 --> 00:02:16.316
our only chance --

00:02:16.316 --> 00:02:19.476
not just to stop the problem
getting worse,

00:02:19.476 --> 00:02:20.596
but to make it better.

00:02:21.716 --> 00:02:24.756
Because even 1.5 degrees
isn't actually safe.

00:02:24.996 --> 00:02:28.596
We’re not there yet, and we’re already
experiencing the fires and the floods

00:02:28.596 --> 00:02:30.156
and the droughts and the storms.

00:02:30.676 --> 00:02:33.076
With carbon removals,

00:02:33.076 --> 00:02:36.756
we can take our historical emissions
out of the atmosphere.

00:02:37.716 --> 00:02:41.236
We can clean up the mess we’ve made,

00:02:41.236 --> 00:02:44.636
and we can give our world
a chance to heal.

00:02:47.436 --> 00:02:49.076
So what do the removals look like?

00:02:49.076 --> 00:02:51.836
I’ve been talking to a lot of people
about carbon removals,

00:02:51.836 --> 00:02:55.316
from business executives
to climate activists,

00:02:55.316 --> 00:02:57.036
and they tend to go one of two ways.

00:02:57.036 --> 00:02:59.556
They either think of trees

00:02:59.556 --> 00:03:02.516
or they think of big, futuristic machines.

00:03:02.676 --> 00:03:04.516
Nature versus tech;

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:06.556
green versus chrome.

00:03:08.036 --> 00:03:10.036
But the more I’ve dug
into carbon removals,

00:03:10.036 --> 00:03:13.636
the more I’ve realized this is not
the right way to think about them.

00:03:13.636 --> 00:03:16.636
Because the world
is full of ways to store carbon.

00:03:16.876 --> 00:03:18.236
You can store it in trees,

00:03:18.236 --> 00:03:19.516
you can store it in soils,

00:03:19.516 --> 00:03:20.956
you can store it in the ocean,

00:03:20.956 --> 00:03:22.396
you can store it in buildings,

00:03:22.396 --> 00:03:23.636
you can store it in rocks,

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.396
you can store it deep underground.

00:03:25.756 --> 00:03:30.796
And every one of those approaches requires
some combination of natural resources

00:03:30.796 --> 00:03:32.716
and human ingenuity

00:03:32.716 --> 00:03:33.716
and technology.

00:03:34.516 --> 00:03:37.836
We’re going to need
both nature and technology.

00:03:38.996 --> 00:03:40.236
I'll show you what I mean.

00:03:40.876 --> 00:03:43.996
So we start on the natural
end of the spectrum with trees.

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:47.116
Now, I still find it astonishing

00:03:47.116 --> 00:03:50.996
that every single tree and plant on Earth

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:55.836
has made its entire body
directly from carbon in the air.

00:03:57.196 --> 00:03:59.476
They’re incredible
carbon capture machines,

00:03:59.476 --> 00:04:01.476
and we’re going to need
a lot more of them.

00:04:01.876 --> 00:04:04.756
But we also have to do it right,

00:04:04.756 --> 00:04:07.916
because if you put trees
in the wrong place ...

00:04:07.916 --> 00:04:08.916
they burn.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:13.716
We have to put trees in places
where they foster biodiversity,

00:04:13.716 --> 00:04:16.356
where they don’t compete
with food for land

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:20.876
and where science and technology
tell us they are likely to survive

00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:22.556
even in the face of climate change.

00:04:24.476 --> 00:04:26.996
Now, if you don’t want
that CO2 from the burning trees

00:04:26.996 --> 00:04:27.996
to get into the sky,

00:04:27.996 --> 00:04:30.956
you can actually burn the wood
deliberately in a power station,

00:04:30.956 --> 00:04:31.996
capture the CO2

00:04:31.996 --> 00:04:34.196
and bury it in a process known as BECCS,

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:36.196
which also gives you energy as a sideline.

00:04:37.236 --> 00:04:41.676
But if you want to keep
the carbon locked up in the wood,

00:04:41.676 --> 00:04:44.436
there are technological ways
to do that as well,

00:04:44.436 --> 00:04:45.436
like this one.

00:04:45.436 --> 00:04:46.436
Look at this.

00:04:46.756 --> 00:04:49.636
A whole new movement
to use wood as a building material

00:04:49.636 --> 00:04:51.876
in modern, high-rise buildings.

00:04:52.076 --> 00:04:54.236
That’s a good idea
for at least three reasons.

00:04:54.236 --> 00:04:56.956
First of all, it keeps
the carbon locked up in the wood.

00:04:56.956 --> 00:05:00.556
Secondly, it displaces
high-emitting materials like concrete.

00:05:00.556 --> 00:05:03.316
And thirdly, the buildings are gorgeous.

00:05:06.316 --> 00:05:09.756
Now you can also use technology
to help carbon stay locked up in soil.

00:05:09.836 --> 00:05:12.996
Storing carbon in soil
sounds like a good idea,

00:05:12.996 --> 00:05:14.116
and it is a good idea.

00:05:14.636 --> 00:05:17.316
The question is how long
does the carbon stay there?

00:05:17.356 --> 00:05:20.836
Because if you change your agricultural
practices for one year, two years,

00:05:20.836 --> 00:05:21.836
that’s fine.

00:05:21.836 --> 00:05:23.796
But if you ever go back to the old ways,

00:05:23.796 --> 00:05:26.036
the carbon goes back up into the sky.

00:05:26.876 --> 00:05:28.356
That’s why this stuff can help.

00:05:28.436 --> 00:05:29.876
It’s called biochar,

00:05:29.876 --> 00:05:33.916
and you make it by taking wood
and burning it in the absence of oxygen

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:34.916
until you get this.

00:05:34.916 --> 00:05:37.076
It locks the carbon up in the wood,

00:05:37.076 --> 00:05:38.796
and now it can’t burn away.

00:05:39.676 --> 00:05:42.436
Now, it turns out that biochar
is very good for soils.

00:05:42.996 --> 00:05:46.516
In fact, Indigenous people in the Amazon
have been using it for generations

00:05:46.516 --> 00:05:48.276
to improve the quality of their soil.

00:05:48.316 --> 00:05:50.556
We can do that on a much bigger scale,

00:05:50.556 --> 00:05:51.876
improve the soil

00:05:51.876 --> 00:05:52.876
and lock up carbon.

00:05:52.876 --> 00:05:54.236
That’s nature

00:05:54.236 --> 00:05:55.756
and technology

00:05:55.756 --> 00:05:57.316
and Indigenous wisdom,

00:05:57.316 --> 00:06:00.116
all in one sweet package.

00:06:00.836 --> 00:06:03.796
Now you can use technology as well ...

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.716
to speed up natural processes.

00:06:06.196 --> 00:06:07.196
So this is basalt.

00:06:07.196 --> 00:06:08.236
It’s a volcanic rock.

00:06:08.236 --> 00:06:10.796
You can find it more or less
everywhere on Earth,

00:06:10.796 --> 00:06:14.516
and it naturally takes up CO2
in a process called chemical weathering.

00:06:14.516 --> 00:06:17.716
Unfortunately, it takes
thousands of years to do it.

00:06:18.716 --> 00:06:21.716
But you can speed up the process
if you grind up the rock

00:06:21.716 --> 00:06:23.196
and spread it on fields,

00:06:23.196 --> 00:06:25.796
and then you can speed it up
to just a couple of years.

00:06:26.596 --> 00:06:28.556
And like biochar, it’s good for the soil.

00:06:28.556 --> 00:06:30.876
You get minerals in the soil
where you need them.

00:06:30.876 --> 00:06:32.596
And when it washes off into the sea,

00:06:32.596 --> 00:06:36.036
it can also help with another
big bugbear of climate change:

00:06:36.036 --> 00:06:37.236
ocean acidification.

00:06:37.516 --> 00:06:41.316
And if we did this on two-thirds
of the world’s croplands,

00:06:41.316 --> 00:06:44.596
we could potentially take
up to four billion tonnes of carbon

00:06:44.596 --> 00:06:46.476
from the atmosphere every year,

00:06:46.476 --> 00:06:48.476
which is a very big chunk of what we need.

00:06:50.356 --> 00:06:51.756
Now, these approaches so far,

00:06:51.756 --> 00:06:52.756
like planting trees,

00:06:52.756 --> 00:06:55.276
take a lot of land
to be able to get them to scale.

00:06:55.756 --> 00:06:57.236
But there is one approach

00:06:57.236 --> 00:07:00.236
where you can actually do it
in a very small amount of land,

00:07:00.236 --> 00:07:01.596
but still get to scale.

00:07:02.156 --> 00:07:04.596
It’s called direct air capture,

00:07:04.596 --> 00:07:07.796
and it basically involves
massive electric fans blowing a lot of air

00:07:07.796 --> 00:07:09.276
over a carbon capture device.

00:07:09.516 --> 00:07:12.676
Now, OK, this does look 
like a big, futuristic machine.

00:07:12.716 --> 00:07:13.716
I admit that.

00:07:13.716 --> 00:07:18.236
But it’s still marrying technology
with a natural process --

00:07:18.236 --> 00:07:19.236
geology --

00:07:19.236 --> 00:07:20.996
because when you’ve captured the CO2,

00:07:20.996 --> 00:07:22.396
you bury it deep underground.

00:07:22.596 --> 00:07:25.556
This is an artist’s impression
of a plant that’s being designed

00:07:25.556 --> 00:07:27.596
by Carbon Engineering in Texas.

00:07:27.916 --> 00:07:29.876
And its aim is that it will take up

00:07:29.876 --> 00:07:32.916
one million tonnes of CO2
from the atmosphere every year.

00:07:33.436 --> 00:07:34.636
And I love this bit --

00:07:34.636 --> 00:07:35.756
where does it go?

00:07:35.876 --> 00:07:39.796
They put it deep underground
in the same geological formations

00:07:39.796 --> 00:07:42.556
that the Texas oil
was originally squeezed out of.

00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:43.996
I love the poetry of this.

00:07:44.356 --> 00:07:45.756
It’s like reversing the valve

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:48.076
and putting the CO2
back where it came from.

00:07:48.916 --> 00:07:51.636
And recent studies have shown
that if you do that,

00:07:51.636 --> 00:07:55.716
98 percent of it will still
be there in 10,000 years.

00:07:56.116 --> 00:07:57.396
When you put it down,

00:07:57.396 --> 00:07:58.396
it stays down.

00:07:59.276 --> 00:08:01.116
This is still a little bit futuristic.

00:08:01.116 --> 00:08:03.236
It’s still in the planning
and design stages,

00:08:03.236 --> 00:08:06.796
but this carbon capture plant
started operating this year.

00:08:07.036 --> 00:08:08.036
A smaller scale,

00:08:08.036 --> 00:08:09.276
but it’s already doing it.

00:08:09.276 --> 00:08:12.116
This is Climeworks and Carbfix in Iceland.

00:08:12.476 --> 00:08:15.596
They're using geothermal heat to power it,

00:08:15.596 --> 00:08:18.876
and they’re putting the CO2
into the Icelandic basaltic rock.

00:08:21.076 --> 00:08:24.676
So one issue, though,
with direct air capture,

00:08:24.676 --> 00:08:26.916
is it takes a lot of energy
to power those fans.

00:08:27.916 --> 00:08:29.916
And that energy obviously has to be green.

00:08:29.956 --> 00:08:30.956
It has to be clean;

00:08:30.956 --> 00:08:32.596
you can’t use fossil fuels for it.

00:08:32.636 --> 00:08:36.476
But it also makes it much more expensive
than other techniques.

00:08:36.716 --> 00:08:39.396
And it takes energy to cook wood
and to grind rock,

00:08:39.396 --> 00:08:41.716
and so all of those approaches
I’m talking about

00:08:41.716 --> 00:08:44.836
are currently much more expensive
than planting trees.

00:08:46.476 --> 00:08:48.316
But we can do something about that.

00:08:48.756 --> 00:08:50.316
We can invest in it now,

00:08:50.316 --> 00:08:51.796
do the research and development

00:08:51.796 --> 00:08:54.476
and get them down the cost curve
to make them affordable.

00:08:54.516 --> 00:08:55.796
It worked for solar power;

00:08:55.796 --> 00:08:56.916
it worked for wind;

00:08:56.916 --> 00:08:58.716
it can work for carbon removals, too.

00:08:59.596 --> 00:09:02.836
Remember, we have to get
to very big scale:

00:09:02.836 --> 00:09:06.076
billions of tonnes
of carbon removals by 2050.

00:09:06.476 --> 00:09:10.956
But we can all help by reducing
our emissions as much as possible

00:09:10.956 --> 00:09:13.116
and figuring out how to remove the rest.

00:09:13.756 --> 00:09:15.516
I’m not talking conventional offsets.

00:09:15.516 --> 00:09:17.716
You know, I put a tonne of CO2 in the air,

00:09:17.716 --> 00:09:20.356
and I pay you not to put
your tonne in the air,

00:09:20.356 --> 00:09:21.716
but my tonne’s still there.

00:09:22.556 --> 00:09:25.916
No, what I mean is I put
a tonne of CO2 in the air,

00:09:25.916 --> 00:09:30.916
I pay someone to take a tonne
out of the air, now or in the near future,

00:09:30.916 --> 00:09:33.356
and we can get to zero and beyond.

00:09:34.356 --> 00:09:38.236
What goes up must now come down.

00:09:39.916 --> 00:09:41.276
And if we all do that,

00:09:41.276 --> 00:09:42.756
if we all make that commitment,

00:09:42.756 --> 00:09:45.956
then the people trying to make
removals happen will have the finance

00:09:45.956 --> 00:09:46.996
and the courage

00:09:46.996 --> 00:09:50.396
and the confidence to be able
to get to the scale that we need.

00:09:51.956 --> 00:09:54.796
I don’t know which of all
of these approaches,

00:09:54.796 --> 00:09:55.796
in the end,

00:09:55.796 --> 00:09:57.996
are going to be the ones
that we’ll have to use

00:09:57.996 --> 00:09:59.476
to get us to the scale we need.

00:09:59.956 --> 00:10:04.476
But I do know that we’re going to need
a whole spread of them,

00:10:04.476 --> 00:10:08.036
with nature and technology
working hand in hand

00:10:08.036 --> 00:10:10.476
to take CO2 out of the sky

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:11.476
and keep it out.

00:10:12.916 --> 00:10:15.076
Which brings me neatly back ...

00:10:15.076 --> 00:10:16.356
to the woolly pigs.

00:10:16.636 --> 00:10:17.636
(Laughter)

00:10:18.276 --> 00:10:21.436
Now, these woolly pigs live
on an estate on the Isle of Mull,

00:10:21.436 --> 00:10:24.076
which is owned by Jim Mann’s
Future Forest Company.

00:10:25.476 --> 00:10:27.996
And I know they don’t look very dangerous,

00:10:27.996 --> 00:10:30.116
but they are actually
the closest you can get

00:10:30.116 --> 00:10:32.316
to a native Scottish wild boar

00:10:32.316 --> 00:10:34.716
without having to have
a dangerous animals license.

00:10:35.036 --> 00:10:38.916
And they do what the Scottish
wild boars used to do.

00:10:39.636 --> 00:10:42.876
They rootle up the undergrowth
left over from selective grazing.

00:10:43.276 --> 00:10:45.796
They let the seed bank
come to the surface

00:10:45.796 --> 00:10:48.356
so that native trees can start to grow

00:10:48.356 --> 00:10:50.276
and start to take up CO2 from the sky.

00:10:52.516 --> 00:10:54.836
They're also planting trees
in this lovely place.

00:10:54.836 --> 00:10:57.876
Native broadleaf like oak
and this beech tree,

00:10:57.876 --> 00:11:00.116
and it’s plenty wet enough
on the Isle of Mull

00:11:00.116 --> 00:11:01.676
that they’re not likely to burn.

00:11:01.676 --> 00:11:03.516
So that’s so far, so natural

00:11:05.156 --> 00:11:08.036
But they also have machines.

00:11:09.196 --> 00:11:12.836
This one is getting ready to cook wood
for biochar to go on the soils,

00:11:12.836 --> 00:11:14.996
and they’ve also got a quarry with basalt.

00:11:14.996 --> 00:11:16.436
They’re quarrying basalt rock.

00:11:16.436 --> 00:11:19.036
They’ve got machines to grind up the rock,

00:11:19.036 --> 00:11:21.476
and they’ve got machines
to spread it on the fields.

00:11:21.476 --> 00:11:26.196
That's three carbon removals approaches
in one beautiful place.

00:11:28.116 --> 00:11:30.396
Now, it’s just one estate,

00:11:30.396 --> 00:11:33.476
but all of these approaches
and more are hugely scalable.

00:11:33.476 --> 00:11:36.316
The world is full of places
where you can plant trees

00:11:36.316 --> 00:11:37.756
and put carbon in soil

00:11:37.756 --> 00:11:39.436
and grind up rock and spread it

00:11:39.436 --> 00:11:40.916
and capture CO2 from the air

00:11:40.916 --> 00:11:42.836
and find places to store it underground.

00:11:43.076 --> 00:11:44.076
All of those things.

00:11:44.076 --> 00:11:45.196
And there’s hundreds --

00:11:45.196 --> 00:11:47.436
thousands of people working right now

00:11:47.436 --> 00:11:52.036
to get those carbon removals approaches
out of the textbook

00:11:52.036 --> 00:11:53.676
and into practice,

00:11:53.676 --> 00:11:55.076
and to help us --

00:11:55.076 --> 00:11:57.156
God help us --

00:11:57.156 --> 00:12:01.316
reverse the damage we have done

00:12:01.316 --> 00:12:05.916
and start the process of climate healing.

00:12:07.916 --> 00:12:09.756
And when I sat on that hillside,

00:12:09.756 --> 00:12:11.956
and I saw the pigs rootling

00:12:11.956 --> 00:12:13.116
and the trees growing

00:12:13.116 --> 00:12:16.156
and the machines
getting ready to cook and grind,

00:12:16.156 --> 00:12:19.036
I loved it ...

00:12:19.036 --> 00:12:21.276
because I experienced an emotion

00:12:21.276 --> 00:12:23.916
that those of us who work
on climate change

00:12:23.916 --> 00:12:26.236
very rarely experience.

00:12:27.076 --> 00:12:29.356
I felt hope.

00:12:30.716 --> 00:12:31.716
Thank you.

00:12:31.716 --> 00:12:38.076
(Applause and cheers)

